The piece of Palantir content that actually stuck with me was this really simple explainer on Ontology.

And basically, the way I understood it was — databases are really good at storing rows and columns, but that’s not really how we think about the world. We think about things like a customer, an order, an aircraft part, and how all of those things are connected.

And honestly, that reminded me a lot of what I was dealing with at UIDAI.

When I built my queue management system, I initially thought of it as, okay, I’m just storing appointments and generating tokens. But once it was actually being used, I realized it wasn’t really about the rows in the database.

You had residents, tokens, offices with different environment, staff, priorities for different people, queues — all of these things interacting with each other. And the system had to reflect those relationships for the administrators and manager for better decision making., not just store transactions. 

That’s what really clicked for me about Palantir.

The interesting engineering problem  that, “How do I structure the system so it actually understands the problem I’m trying to solve?”

And that’s the kind of problem I really want to keep working on.
